# Android-only Tark patches (A1), applied after Tark's shared patches. They
# live outside patches/tarka/ so the Mac and Windows series never see them.
#
# ungoogled's enable-extra-locales.patch (desktop) deletes Chromium's Android
# branch in build/config/locales.gni, which breaks gn on Android
# (components/strings removes "cy", which is then in neither list). Only the
# extended_locales half comes back: the same patch makes Android's
# locale_settings grd emit Welsh paks, so Welsh stays in platform_pak_locales.
locales-android-branch.patch
#
# ungoogled builds chrome/browser/safe_browsing without Safe Browsing: the
# target's base block is `if (false)` and defines no deps, so its is_android
# block's `deps +=` is an undefined identifier on Android. That block (Safe
# Browsing's Android UI and API pieces) is off as well.
safe-browsing-no-android-deps.patch
#
# ungoogled's add-suggestions-url-field.patch gives ResetTemplateURL a
# suggestions_url argument, which it writes over the engine's own; Android's
# callers (desktop never compiles them) pass the engine's existing one, so
# they behave as upstream.
search-engines-android-suggestions-url.patch
#
# ungoogled's disable-untraceable-urls.patch removes Google's RLZ server
# (rlz_lib::kFinancialServer), which Android's RLZ ping handler uses. Tark
# sends no RLZ pings: the JNI entry reports failure without a request.
rlz-ping-handler-no-ping.patch
#
# Android-only code that still names Safe Browsing pieces ungoogled removes
# (safe_browsing_mode=0): download obfuscation's deep-scan check, the
# proceed-anyway pref in interstitial reporting, suspicious-notification UKM,
# the Safe Browsing page's deep-scan log, and an include the removed prefs
# header used to bring in.
safe-browsing-android-leftovers.patch
#
# ungoogled's add-flag-to-configure-extension-downloading.patch includes
# extensions/browser/extension_util.h unconditionally; its one use is inside
# ENABLE_EXTENSIONS_CORE, which Android does not build.
download-extension-util-include.patch
#
# libchrome.so's link: Android's Java UI declares Safe Browsing natives
# (SafeBrowsingBridge and two dialog bridges) and interstitials link the
# settings launchers, none of which ungoogled builds. A Tark file gives them
# stand-ins that read Safe Browsing as off; the remaining callers of removed
# code (suspicious-notification UKM, the tips agent's enhanced-protection
# check and, since 156, the tips feature's enhanced-protection tip, password
# reuse detection) drop or guard the call.
safe-browsing-android-link.patch
#
# Direct APK ≤ 195 MB (the user's decisions: 130 MB on 2026-10-20, then 180
# and 182 MB for the ad blocker, 195 MB on 2026-10-22 for live captions'
# ONNX Runtime): libchrome.so is
# stored compressed and extracted at install (Chromium stores it
# uncompressed: 317 MB), and the XR module (Android XR headsets, ARCore) is
# left out.
direct-apk-size.patch
#
# ungoogled never calls ProfileOAuth2TokenService::LoadCredentials, so the
# Android token service's account seeding (the device's Google accounts, from
# Java at startup) hit its CHECK and crashed every start. Tark uses no Google
# accounts: seeding is skipped when credentials were never loaded.
signin-android-no-account-seeding.patch
#
# Chromium 156 takes version_info's version on Android from the APK's
# version name. Tark's ("156.0.8078.47-r1", android/release/version.py) is
# not a base::Version, so variations setup hit a CHECK at every start; the
# version is Chromium's again, as on desktop.
version-info-chromium-version.patch
#
# ungoogled stops registering the sign-in prefs; Android's first run reads
# signin.allowed and PrefService CHECKs on an unregistered pref. It is
# registered again on Android, false: sign-in is not allowed.
signin-android-allowed-pref.patch
#
# Google contacts A1's emulator run found that the Mac build never makes:
# the first-run variations seed (Java, clientservices.googleapis.com, outside
# the net log), FCM tokens and IDs from Play Services (policy invalidations at
# startup; Web Push uses the same path), Widevine provisioning at startup
# (www.googleapis.com), and the new tab page's built-in tiles, which fetch
# icons from freenode.net, github.com and others.
google-callers-off.patch
#
# The launcher label and widget titles are Android resources, which Tark's
# name substitution does not reach: Chromium becomes Tark.
app-name-tark.patch
#
# Home as on the Mac (the user's request, 2026-10-20): a new tab opens
# Shodhya Today (shodhya.com) instead of Android's own page, except in
# Incognito and offline; no first-run welcome.
home-shodhya-today.patch
#
# ungoogled builds no sync service, so SyncServiceFactory gives null on
# Android; the tab switcher's tab groups list registered a listener on it and
# crashed when opened (seen on a phone). It now reads sync as off.
tab-groups-without-sync.patch
#
# A4 (spec §13): the Mac's Paper palette at its source — Chromium's colour
# ramps retuned (indigo primary, warm neutrals, paper and raised surfaces), the
# theme's text, background and hairline roles on Tark's tokens — and no
# dynamic colour, so Tark looks the same on every phone.
look-paper-palette.patch
#
# A4: the mockup's address bar — no home or profile button, the mark as the
# menu button (MenuButton took the icon as a bitmap; it now takes the vector),
# the mark instead of Shodhya's favicon (a dark square in private mode, the
# user's report), and a hairline round the address pill.
look-address-bar.patch
#
# A4: the Tark app icon — the mark on dark paper as the adaptive launcher
# icon, and the mark as Android's themed (monochrome) icon. The in-app copies
# (mipmap app_icon) come from resources/branding/mark/install.txt.
look-app-icon.patch
#
# A4: the menu as the Mac's Tark sheet for a phone — New tab, New private
# tab, History and Downloads as tiles (each tile is the old item's own model);
# no Delete browsing data (it stays in History and Settings), Recent tabs
# (sync), Customize new tab page or Help & feedback (Google).
look-menu.patch
#
# A4: the tab switcher on paper — the palette does most of it; here the pane
# switcher (Tabs / Private / Groups) becomes a full pill and other tabs'
# cards raised paper.
look-tab-switcher.patch
#
# The user: "we want incognito beside the group tab at top". The tab
# switcher's Private button (Tabs / Private / Groups) is always there while
# the profile allows private tabs, not only while some are open; with none,
# its pane shows empty and its new tab button opens a private tab. Closing the
# last private tab still returns to Tabs.
private-tab-button.patch
#
# A4: Settings as the Mac's — a header with the summary line ("Private by
# default. No Google · no sync", English and Hindi) and tiles that open the
# rows below; no sign-in, Google services or Safety check rows. The plain
# words ("Passwords", "Site permissions", …) come from name substitution.
# Every settings screen draws hairline dividers between rows.
look-settings.patch
#
# A2: adblock-rust 0.13.3 in Chromium's Rust crates. Chromium's Cargo.toml,
# Cargo.lock and gnrt_config.toml gain adblock and its ten new dependencies
# (configured as brave-core does), with the BUILD.gn files gnrt generates for
# them; regex, which Chromium ships only to tests, becomes shippable; bitflags
# and serde gain the features adblock asks for. The crates' sources are put in
# place by android/adblock/crates.py (pinned sha256), with two crate patches
# recorded here: adblock uses Chromium's thiserror 2 and drops the url crate
# (one unused error conversion); seahash gets the MIT licence it declares.
adblock-crates.patch
#
# A2: the ad blocker's engine. Brave's cxx bridge to adblock-rust (trimmed),
# its domain resolver and redirect stub response go to third_party/
# brave_adblock (MPL-2.0); Tark's service compiles the newest list bundle
# (the APK's asset or a verified download) on its own sequence, caches the
# compiled engine, and blocks nothing until it is ready. Its updater fetches
# the daily signed bundle from gettarka.com (a day on Wi-Fi, three on mobile
# data); the bundle and update rules have unit tests built for the build
# machine (android/build.sh runs them).
adblock-engine.patch
#
# A2: Android has no extensions, so uBlock Origin's resource pak (Tark's
# shared patches add it on every platform) is left out of Android's paks; the
# engine-level blocker replaces it.
adblock-no-ublock-pak.patch
#
# A2: ad requests blocked before they leave. A proxying URLLoaderFactory
# (modelled on Chromium's signin one) sits in front of every page, frame and
# worker factory; the engine's answer fails a request as uBlock Origin does
# (ERR_BLOCKED_BY_CLIENT, element collapsed) or serves its $redirect stub, and
# redirects are checked again. Frames' WebSockets are checked too. A site the
# person turned blocking off for (ADS = allow) passes untouched.
adblock-network.patch
#
# A2: hiding rules and scriptlets. Each frame asks the browser (synchronously,
# at commit) for its page's resources; at document start, before the page's
# own scripts, the scriptlets run in the page's world and the hide selectors
# become a user stylesheet; a collector in an isolated world reports class
# and id names, and their generic hide rules follow.
adblock-cosmetic.patch
#
# A2: "Block ads on this site" in the page info sheet (English and Hindi);
# off stores ADS = allow for the site, which the proxy and the cosmetic
# resources read; the page reloads.
adblock-site-switch.patch
#
# The Mac's "What Tark connects to" (spec §5.3) in Privacy and security:
# Shodhya, and gettarka.com for the ad blocker's lists, in plain words,
# English and Hindi.
look-connects.patch
#
# The Mac's offline new tab page (spec §13, left for later at A4): online a
# new tab opens Shodhya Today, so Android's own new tab page only shows
# offline. It becomes the Mac's page on paper: the mark, the search pill
# (raised, a rule hairline, no shadow, the search loupe) and "You're offline.
# Searches will run when you're back." (English and Hindi) while there is no
# connection; the page is paper in light mode too (Chromium's home surface
# colour was raised); no tiles, tips cards or theme sheet.
look-offline-new-tab.patch
#
# Privacy and security without Google leftovers, as on the Mac: no Safe
# Browsing row ("Safe Browsing is off": ungoogled builds none), no "Warn you if
# a password was compromised" switch (the check asks Google), no "go to Google
# Services" footer. The rows leave privacy_preferences.xml, so settings search
# no longer finds them; Safe Browsing's own page, with no row leading to it,
# drops out of the search index as an orphan.
privacy-no-google-rows.patch
#
# Page info for Tark's own pages (about:blank, chrome://) shows Tark's mark in
# its colours instead of Chromium's tinted logo.
look-page-info-mark.patch
#
# A3 (spec §5): the Mac's Indian-language rows on Android. The model and word
# lists are downloaded into the app's data directory, one word-list file per
# language (android/xlit/files.py); tark_xlit reads them from there once they
# are in place, and its unit tests run on the build machine.
xlit-android-files.patch
#
# A3: the provider runs on Android too; until the files are in place it adds
# one row that offers them ("Type in हिन्दी?"). Java shows an Indian-language
# row's language at the right edge, as the Mac does, and opens the offer.
xlit-android-rows.patch
#
# A3: the download. The model and the word lists of the person's languages
# come from gettarka.com only after they say yes (the offer row's dialog or
# Settings), on Wi-Fi unless they choose "Download now"; each file is checked
# against the size and sha256 compiled into the APK.
xlit-android-download.patch
#
# A3: Settings > Languages > Indian-language suggestions: on downloads (on
# Wi-Fi), off stops the rows and keeps the files.
xlit-android-settings.patch
#
# A3: Android's keyword chip (Chromium's site search) is off by default; on,
# Tark's @ sources open a chip and search that site, as on the Mac.
omnibox-site-search-on.patch
#
# A3 (the user: "add divider in all screens in settings"): ten screens turned
# A4's hairline off for themselves (Theme, Image descriptions, Accessibility,
# the site-settings screens); the one place that reads that choice now draws
# it on every screen.
look-settings-dividers.patch
#
# A3 (the user: "make sure it aligns with back button and question mark from
# header"): Chromium's containment styling adds a 16dp side margin around
# settings rows on top of their own padding; without it, rows line up with
# the toolbar.
look-settings-padding.patch
#
# A3 (the user: "Open Tark help on gettarka.com"): help and feedback opened
# Google's Chrome help; they open Tark's help page, the screen's section.
look-help-on-gettarka.patch
#
# A3 (the user: fix the Google Translate row): Translate is off without a
# server (ungoogled-chromium), so its settings go unless one is given, as on the Mac.
translate-settings-off.patch
#
# Faster Indian-language rows (the user, 2026-10-21): a decoder step's
# products of one to four rows read each weight row once and take dot
# products, instead of Eigen packing the whole weight matrix on every call
# (41% of a word's time on Android).
xlit-small-products.patch
#
# A5: the direct APK's daily update check. Tark's index on gettarka.com, not
# Google's update service, feeds the app menu's "Update Tark" item; off when
# Google Play installed Tark.
update-check.patch
#
# r2: websites can't share the screen in Tark for Android, so the capture
# service neither declares nor asks for the media projection type.
media-no-projection.patch
# r2: Tark's three dots, not Chrome's logo, on the media notification.
look-media-icon.patch
#
# Live captions (spec: Android captions; Phase 1, videos). The engine is the
# Mac's components/tark_captions, generated from the captions patches by
# android/captions/engine.py (a test keeps the two identical), with the root
# gn_all naming its host tests so that gn loads it.
captions-engine.patch
#
# Live captions: ONNX Runtime's arm64 .so in the APK, compressed and
# extracted at install like libchrome.so (34.2 MB, the user's decision).
captions-ort.patch
#
# Live captions: the model downloads. The files of each unit (common, then
# one per language) are pinned by size and sha256 in the APK
# (android/captions/files.json); their rules (check, scan, removal while in
# use) have no browser dependencies and are tested on the build machine.
# A unit two features want (the language detector, language_id: captions and
# voice search) stays until neither does (tark.captions.owners).
captions-downloads.patch
#
# Live captions: the captions process. "Tark captions", a sandboxed utility
# process per captioning tab, runs the engine on the model files the browser
# hands over (it opens nothing itself); CaptionSession is tested on the build
# machine.
captions-service.patch
#
# Live captions: a tab's media audio. Chromium's speech-recognition hook in
# the media player (desktop Live Caption's) is built on Android too; Tark's
# client sends the audio to the browser only while captions are on in the
# tab. Players Android runs itself (HLS, casting) are not reachable.
captions-audio.patch
#
# Live captions: each tab's captions (TarkCaptionsTab: players, visibility,
# the downloads, the captions process), decided by TabCaptions (tested on the
# build machine), and the bridge to the caption bar.
captions-tab.patch
#
# Live captions: the caption bar (the mockup: the mark · Captions · the
# language · ✕, the first download, the captions) in the bottom container,
# Settings › Accessibility › Live captions (caption style, languages on the
# phone), the "What Tark connects to" row, English and Hindi.
captions-ui.patch
#
# Voice (spec: Android voice): the Web Speech API on the device only. Android's
# on-device recognizer (Android 12+), never the online one; below 12 or
# without it, start() ends with service-not-allowed. Every Android error ends
# the session with the spec's name (never network); a missing language is
# language-not-supported and tells the language delegate.
speech-on-device.patch
#
# Voice: a missing language shows a snackbar offering Android's own download
# (named and downloaded on Android 13+, voice input settings on 12), only on tap.
speech-language-snackbar.patch
#
# Voice: the language the person chose for a site ("Use … next time"). The
# browser passes the frame's origin (scheme://host:port only) to the Java
# recognizer, which starts in the site's language when the phone has it (or
# Android hasn't said), else in the page's and tells the language delegate
# the remembered one is missing. The page sees nothing new.
speech-site-language.patch
#
# Voice: Settings > Languages > Voice languages (Android 13+; a pointer to
# Android's voice settings on 12): languages on the phone, suggested from the
# phone's languages, Tark's languages and captions (with the reason), and
# more, each downloaded by Android on a tap. The snackbar and the setting
# share VoicePlatform. Sites: each site's chosen language (VoiceSiteLanguages,
# phone-only preferences), with Remove. After the first voice session, a
# one-time offer of the suggested languages (VoiceLanguageOffer, M3; "No
# thanks" or Download ends it). Spoken-language detection's row gets or
# removes the detector through captions' downloads (VoiceDetector). English
# and Hindi.
voice-languages.patch
#
# Voice: spoken-language detection (Android 13+, a single utterance, only
# when the person turned it on and Tark holds RECORD_AUDIO). Tark captures
# the microphone and feeds Android's recognizer through a pipe
# (EXTRA_AUDIO_SOURCE) as the audio arrives; captions' detector
# (language_id) reads the same audio from a 15 s ring held in memory only.
# The page's results are unchanged: text from the partial results, segments
# joined, no text is no-speech. A different language heard: "Sounds like
# Hindi · Switch" (replays the ring into a recognizer in Hindi), "That
# sounded like Hindi · Use Hindi next time", or the missing-language
# snackbar naming it. Nothing changes without the person's tap; private
# tabs never store a site language.
voice-language-detect.patch
